{"claims":[{"excerpt":"Yellowstonewolven en veranderende prooirelaties","claim":"Yellowstone wolves’ prey relationships changed over time.","category":"other","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"That ratio reversed from 2001 to 2007, indicating changes in prey vulnerability and availability.","narrative":"de verschuiving in verhouding tussen elandkalveren en volwassen mannelijke elanden onder de vroege-winterprooien in 1995–2000 en 2001–2007","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The title’s reference to changing prey relationships is supported by the reported reversal in the proportions of two prey classes.","materiality":"The change is central to the piece."},{"excerpt":"Yellowstonewolven leven in sociale familiegroepen. Ze jagen samen en zorgen gezamenlijk voor hun jongen.","claim":"Yellowstone wolves live in social family groups, hunt together, and cooperatively care for their young.","category":"entity","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The pack is a complex social family"; "cooperative care of offspring, group hunting of large prey","narrative":"Introduceer Yellowstonewolven als sociale familiegroepen, met onder meer gezamenlijke jacht en zorg voor jongen.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source describes packs as social families and identifies group hunting and cooperative offspring care.","materiality":"These details establish the wolves’ social behavior."},{"excerpt":"Ook na een wolvendoding reikt die sociale rol verder","claim":"The wolves’ social role extends to the ecological relationships formed around their kills.","category":"interpretation","label":"F1 minor immaterial imprecision","evidence":"Many other animals benefit from wolf kills.","narrative":"Yellowstonewolven zijn sociale roofdieren: ze jagen in groepen en hun prooien voeden ook andere dieren.","narrativeRelation":"ambiguous","origin":"new_distortion","explanation":"The source supports social pack behavior and separately describes animals benefiting from wolf kills, but does not characterize the latter as an extension of wolves’ social role. The wording joins those ideas; the following food-web claim itself is supported.","materiality":"A minor framing issue that does not materially change the account of the food relationships."},{"excerpt":"de prooi levert voedsel aan onder meer raven, coyotes, beren en ongewervelden.","claim":"Wolf-kill prey provides food to ravens, coyotes, bears, and invertebrates, among others.","category":"entity","label":"U insufficient evidence to settle","evidence":"Ravens and magpies arrive almost immediately. Coyotes arrive soon after, waiting nearby until the wolves are sated. Bears will attempt to chase the wolves away, and are usually successful. Many other animals—from eagles to invertebrates—consume the remains.","narrative":"Laat zien dat een wolvendoding voedsel biedt aan dieren van raven en coyotes tot beren en ongewervelden.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The source explicitly describes ravens, coyotes, and invertebrates feeding at kills, and says bears benefit by chasing wolves away. It does not explicitly say bears consume the remains, so the complete list-as-food claim cannot be fully settled from the source.","materiality":"The uncertainty concerns one named group in an otherwise source-supported description."},{"excerpt":"De prooien van wolven zijn niet altijd in dezelfde verhouding vertegenwoordigd. Onder de prooien in de vroege winter veranderde tussen 1995–2000 en 2001–2007 de verhouding tussen elandkalveren en volwassen mannelijke elanden.","claim":"The early-winter prey proportions of elk calves and adult male elk changed between 1995–2000 and 2001–2007.","category":"quantity","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"From 1995 to 2000, in early winter, elk calves comprised 50% of wolf prey, and bull elk comprised 25%. That ratio reversed from 2001 to 2007","narrative":"Gebruik daarnaast de verschuiving in de verhouding tussen elandkalveren en volwassen mannelijke elanden onder de vroege-winterprooien in 1995–2000 en 2001–2007 als concreet voorbeeld","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source gives the initial proportions and says the ratio reversed in the later period. The piece does not add unsupported exact later percentages.","materiality":"This is the piece’s specific example of changing prey composition."},{"excerpt":"De waarnemingen tonen zowel de voedselrelaties rond een wolvendoding als veranderingen in predatie.","claim":"The observations demonstrate both food relationships around wolf kills and changes in predation.","category":"outcome","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Many other animals benefit from wolf kills."; "Changes in wolf predation patterns and impacts on prey species like elk are inextricably linked to other factors","narrative":"Breng de twee observaties samen: wolven maken deel uit van sociale en voedselrelaties, terwijl veranderingen in predatie","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"Both the kill-related food relationships and changes in wolf predation are described in the source.","materiality":"This sentence summarizes the two main observations."},{"excerpt":"De effecten op elanden vallen samen met andere factoren, waaronder andere roofdieren, het beheer van hoefdieren buiten het park en het weer.","claim":"Effects on elk coincide with other factors, including other predators, ungulate management outside the park, and weather.","category":"cause","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Changes in wolf predation patterns and impacts on prey species like elk are inextricably linked to other factors, such as other predators, management of ungulates outside the park, and weather","narrative":"de effecten op elanden samenvallen met andere factoren, waaronder andere roofdieren, beheer van hoefdieren buiten het park en het weer.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The piece accurately reports the listed factors and uses the cautious relation “coincide”; it does not claim that wolves alone cause the effects.","materiality":"This qualification prevents an overstated single-cause account."}]}

